## Context

The current Phase B loop (ticket 0207, landed in commits leading
to 0185 closure) sends the same generic "Proceed as you think is
best in autonomous agentic mode." reply on every follow-up turn
until budget triggers the terminal reply. The 2026-05-21 multi-turn
smoke showed two failure modes of this policy:

- Turn 1 produced a usable report. Turn 2 sent the generic
  encouragement — but the right reply was "verify and polish what
  you just produced, especially the per-row source columns." We
  wasted a turn (and ended up triggering the model to *plan* an
  improvement instead of doing one) because the harness didn't
  know what had already been delivered.
- After three generic encouragements with no report, we'd keep
  going indefinitely (only the budget cap stops us). There's no
  graceful "we don't think this is going anywhere" exit short of
  exhaustion.

The locked policy ("two reply slots: standard + terminal") was too
coarse. Replacing it with a small state machine, driven by an LLM
classifier on each assistant reply, gives both stronger steering
and a defensible stop condition.

## State machine (author's spec, 2026-05-21)

Variables tracked per Phase B session:

- `verify_used: bool` — has the one verify/polish round been spent?
- `encouragement_count: int` (0..3) — how many "proceed autonomously"
  replies have we sent without seeing a report
- `remaining_budget_usd: float` — total cap minus spend so far
- `wall_elapsed_s: float` — for the status reminder

After each assistant reply (including turn 1's), classify the reply
via an LLM call (cheap model, see below). Two classes:

- **report**: the assistant produced a structured inventory
  (table + sources + narrative — at least one of those, materially)
- **no_report**: the assistant did not produce a report
  (planning, clarification, refusal, partial intermediate)

Transition rules:

```
if remaining_budget <= BUDGET_TRIGGER_FRAC * cap_usd:
    send TERMINAL_REPLY (with budget status); accept one more
    response; stop. Overrides everything below.

if class == "report":
    if not verify_used:
        send VERIFY_REPLY (with budget status); verify_used = True;
        encouragement_count = 0
    else:
        # we already used our one verify round; this is the polished
        # response. Accept it and stop.
        stop
elif class == "no_report":
    encouragement_count += 1   # increment first, then check (anchored by tests)
    if encouragement_count < MAX_ENCOURAGEMENTS:   # MAX_ENCOURAGEMENTS = 3
        send ENCOURAGE_REPLY (with budget status)
    else:
        # encouragement budget exhausted without a report; graceful exit
        send TERMINAL_REPLY (with budget status); accept one more
        response; stop.
```

(Implementation note clarified post-merge: `encouragement_count` is
incremented *before* the bound check so TERMINAL fires on the third
no_report observation — i.e., total 4 turns for the pure no_report
trace: turn 1 = designed prompt, turns 2–3 = ENCOURAGE, turn 4 =
TERMINAL response accepted, stop. This matches
`test_state_machine_three_no_reports_then_terminal` in
`tests/test_exp2_interactive_smoke.py` and was the anchor verified
during /verify on PR #401.)

Status reminder (chat-text prefix) appears on every user-side
message after turn 1, as in the current policy.

## Three reply strings (verbatim, fixed condition for Annex C)

**ENCOURAGE_REPLY** (≤3 times before forcing terminal):

> "Proceed as you think is best in autonomous agentic mode."

**VERIFY_REPLY** (sent once after the first reply classified as
report):

> "Thank you for the inventory. Please now verify and polish it
> in ONE focused pass, prioritising: (a) per-row provenance — every
> Source 1 and Source 2 cell must point to a specific URL from
> your bibliography; (b) coverage — any plant present in your
> bibliography but absent from the table; (c) temporality — every
> row has an as-of date or status-change note; (d) internal
> consistency — capacity totals reconcile across the table and the
> statistical summary. Return the corrected inventory only — no
> meta-commentary on what you changed."

**TERMINAL_REPLY** (as today):

> "I have no additional directive to give you. Please proceed to
> generating the report without further asking. If you cannot, we
> would appreciate to know why, but the discussion will stop here
> in any case. Thanks for your understanding."

## Classifier

- **Model**: a cheap fast LLM, pinned in `models.yaml` under a new
  family `classifier`. Candidate: `mistral-small-latest` or
  `gpt-5-nano` or `openrouter/openai/gpt-5-mini`. Cost per
  classification: well under $0.001.
- **Surface**: a single function
  `classify_report(narrative: str) -> Literal["report", "no_report"]`
  in `experiments/sota/dialogue_classifier.py`. Same module exposes
  the model id and the prompt as constants for reproducibility.
- **Prompt**: short, structured, demands a single-word answer.
  Example draft:

  > You are a classification assistant. The following is a single
  > response from an AI agent that was asked to produce a structured
  > inventory of Vietnam's thermal power plants (>30MWe). Did this
  > response materially deliver an inventory? Reply with exactly
  > one word: "report" if it includes a structured table or
  > equivalent enumeration of plants, "no_report" if it is
  > planning, clarification, refusal, or a meta-statement about
  > intent without the substance.
  >
  > AGENT RESPONSE:
  > ```
  > {narrative_excerpt_up_to_8K_chars}
  > ```
  >
  > Reply with exactly one word: "report" or "no_report".

- **Failure modes**: classifier 4xx / 5xx / parse-fail (model
  returns something other than the two expected words) → treat as
  `no_report` and log; never crash the smoke.
- **Cost accounting**: classifier cost is **harness overhead**,
  not part of the SOTA agent's $10 budget. Reported separately in
  the per-turn cost artefact under a `classifier_cost_usd` key.

## Per-turn artefacts (extended)

In addition to the current four files per turn, add:

- `{agent}_turn_NN.classification.json` — `{class: "report"|"no_report", classifier_cost_usd: float, classifier_model: str, wall_s: float}`

So the manuscript can later report turn counts AND turn shape
("Mistral: turn 1 = report, turn 2 = verify-polish, turn 3 =
report; closed at turn 3 polished.").

## Actions

1. Add `experiments/sota/dialogue_classifier.py` with the
   `classify_report()` function + unit tests (mocked).
2. Pin the classifier model in `models.yaml` (or as a constant in
   the module, depending on project preference).
3. Replace `run_phase_b_multiturn` with a state-machine
   implementation per the spec above. Keep the same external
   surface (output dir layout, artefacts).
4. Update `format_status_line` / `select_reply` to expose three
   slots instead of two; deprecate the binary terminal-threshold
   logic in favour of the state machine.
5. Unit-test the state machine with mocked adapter and mocked
   classifier — at minimum:
   - report on turn 1 → verify-reply on turn 2 → accept turn 2's
     response and stop
   - no_report ×4 → encourage ×3 → terminal on turn 4 → accept and
     stop
   - budget exhausted mid-flow → terminal overrides regardless
6. Re-smoke Mistral end-to-end with the state machine. Verify a
   verify-polish round actually fills the per-row source columns
   (which is the test of whether the redesign was worth it).

## Out of scope

- Multi-class classification (just "report" / "no_report").
- Classifying the *quality* of the report (that's evaluation, not
  dialogue control).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] State machine implemented; old two-slot logic removed.
- [ ] Classifier module + tests; classifier cost separated from
      agent cost in artefacts.
- [ ] Re-smoke on Mistral shows: turn-2 verify reply triggered after
      a turn-1 report, polished inventory landed by turn 3.
- [ ] Policy text (verbatim three reply strings + state machine +
      classifier model id) documented for 0199 / Annex C.
